---
title: Predicting antibiotic activity against drug-resistant pneumococcus
org: Imperial College London
period: 2024–2026
role: Second of seven authors. I wrote the modelling code and ran the numerical experiments together with the first author, and selected the candidates for in-vitro testing with the principal investigator. The microbiological experiments were performed and analysed by collaborators at the MRC Centre for Global Infectious Disease Analysis.
---

*Streptococcus pneumoniae* is a priority pathogen for new antibiotics, and resistance to the
existing ones is common. Developing a new drug takes a decade; finding one among drugs that
already exist takes less. The question we set out to answer was whether a model could pick
repurposing candidates well enough that a lab would bother testing them.

The work is published, open access, so the full method and every number is checkable:

> Fitch, J. S.; **Ariadi, M. D.**; Kwun, M. J.; Howells, L.; Hernandez-Hernandez, S.;
> Croucher, N. J.; Ballester, P. J. "Artificial Intelligence-Guided Phenotypic Drug Repurposing
> Against *Streptococcus pneumoniae*." *Advanced Science*, 2026.
> [doi:10.1002/advs.76959](https://doi.org/10.1002/advs.76959) ·
> [full text on Europe PMC](https://europepmc.org/article/MED/42745629)

My working repository for the modelling is
[github.com/mdhyaksa/pneumoccusVS](https://github.com/mdhyaksa/pneumoccusVS). Note that the
paper's own data-availability statement points at the first author's repository, which is the
canonical artefact for the publication.

## The data problem comes first

We assembled 36,352 compounds from ChEMBL and PubChem: 1,849 with measured activity against
*S. pneumoniae*, mostly from assays on erythromycin- and penicillin-resistant strains, and
34,503 inactive.

That ratio is the first thing to be suspicious of, and the inactives are where the judgement
lies. Genuine pneumococcal screening data is scarce, so the bulk of the negative class comes
from a published *S. aureus* growth-inhibition screen, treated as presumed-inactive. That is an
assumption, not a measurement, and the paper justifies it on two grounds: the hit rate in
pneumococcal screens runs around 0.6%, so an untested compound is overwhelmingly likely to be
inactive; and disk-diffusion results for the two species correlate at 0.80 across 47
antibiotic–concentration pairs. We also ablated it — retraining without those presumed
negatives — and reported what changed.

## The split matters more than the model

A molecule that closely resembles its training set is easy to score, and scoring it well tells
you almost nothing about how the model behaves on something new. Random splits over chemical
data produce exactly this illusion, and the resulting metrics are optimistic in a way that does
not survive contact with a lab.

So the evaluation was built to be pessimistic on purpose:

1. **Hold out the structural outliers.** Any molecule whose maximum Tanimoto similarity to
   anything else in the dataset fell below 0.5 was removed to a held-out set — 4,812 molecules.
   These are the compounds the model has least right to be confident about, and they are the
   test set.
2. **Cluster what remains.** UMAP with a Jaccard metric, then agglomerative clustering, with
   *K* chosen by silhouette score over a sweep from 3 to 50. The sweep selected **K = 26**.
   Positive rates across clusters range from 0% to 57%, which is itself a warning about what a
   random split would have mixed together.
3. **Leave one cluster out.** Train an ensemble member per held-out cluster, so every member is
   validated on a scaffold group it never saw.

## Three model families over the same splits

<div class="table-wrap">

| Model | Representation | Shape |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Random forest | 2048-bit Morgan fingerprints, radius 3 | Grid search, 5-fold stratified CV, optimised for PR-AUC |
| D-MPNN | Learned molecular graph + 200 RDKit descriptors | 26-model ensemble, median aggregation |
| MoLFormer | Pre-trained chemical language model | 5-model ensemble, frozen encoder + FFN head |

</div>

The random forest is the baseline worth taking seriously, not a straw man — fingerprints plus
trees remain hard to beat on small, imbalanced chemical datasets. The graph network learns its
own representation. The third used a transformer pre-trained on 111 million SMILES strings with
the encoder frozen, so roughly 80% of its 55 million parameters never updated and only the head
was trained; the paper reports this as the first use of a pre-trained molecular language model
for antibiotic screening.

Everything was tuned against PR-AUC rather than accuracy or ROC-AUC. With a positive class
under 6%, accuracy is uninformative and ROC-AUC is flattering; precision-recall is the metric
that reflects what you care about, which is the quality of the top of the ranking.

## Retrospective results

On the held-out structural outliers, with 49 actives present:

<div class="table-wrap">

| Model or combination | MCC | Precision | Recall | PR-AUC | ROC-AUC |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| MoLFormer ∩ RF | 0.493 | 1.000 | 0.245 | **0.630** | 0.979 |
| MoLFormer ∪ RF | **0.556** | 0.591 | 0.531 | 0.590 | 0.979 |
| Random forest, tuned | 0.536 | 0.889 | 0.327 | 0.526 | 0.963 |
| MoLFormer ensemble | 0.505 | 0.579 | 0.449 | 0.528 | 0.975 |
| D-MPNN ensemble | 0.424 | 0.500 | 0.367 | 0.437 | 0.949 |

</div>

The intersection of the two models buys precision at the cost of recall; the union does the
reverse. For a screen where the downstream cost is a wet-lab assay, the intersection is the
useful end of that trade — but note the ROC-AUC column sitting at 0.95–0.98 for every row,
including the weakest. It is the least discriminating number in the table.

## The prospective test, which is the real one

Retrospective metrics are a rehearsal. We screened 6,747 compounds from the Drug Repurposing
Hub, excluding anything already tested against *S. pneumoniae* in training, took the compounds
scoring highest by consensus and by each model individually, triaged them by hand, and sent 11
to the lab.

**Nine of the 11 strongly reduced growth of *S. pneumoniae* R6, at IC50 ≤ 0.4 µg/mL.**
Thiostrepton came in at 0.0001 µg/mL — 60 picomolar — and ceftiofur at 0.0004 µg/mL. Only one
of the 11, levofloxacin, was already known to be active against this organism. Thiostrepton
held up against three multidrug-resistant strains, at 120 pM.

## Where the models disagreed

This is the part I find most worth presenting. Ranking the 11 tested drugs by model score and
correlating against their measured potency:

<div class="table-wrap">

| Model | Spearman ρ | p |
| --- | --- | --- |
| MoLFormer ensemble | **0.75** | 0.0073 |
| MoLFormer + D-MPNN consensus | 0.74 | 0.0098 |
| D-MPNN ensemble | 0.15 | 0.65 |
| Random forest | 0.16 | 0.63 |

</div>

The language model ranked the compounds in roughly the right order. The random forest did not —
yet it was the forest that ranked levofloxacin 7th, where both deep models buried it around
120th, and the forest put thiostrepton, the most potent compound found, at 115th while the deep
models ranked it 1st. Dactinomycin was 7th for MoLFormer and 192nd for the forest.

No single model would have found every active. The honest conclusion is not that one
architecture won but that they fail in different directions, which is an argument for consensus
screening and against reading too much into a leaderboard.

Two caveats belong next to that table, and they are in the paper: *n* = 11 is a small sample for
a correlation, and the selection process favoured compounds the deep models liked, so the forest
was partly being judged on molecules it did not nominate.

## Interpretability

I ran SHAP over the fingerprint bits, mapped the important bits back onto atoms to see which
substructures drove predictions, and used Monte Carlo tree search to extract minimal active
subgraphs scored by the MoLFormer head. β-lactam and quinolone scaffolds showed up as expected,
which is mostly a sanity check that the models learned known chemistry rather than an artefact.

One note for anyone reading the repository: the notebook is named `pneumococcus_causal.ipynb`,
but there is no causal inference in it. It is attribution and interpretability work. The name
is a leftover and it oversells what the analysis does.

## Stack

PyTorch, PyTorch Lightning, scikit-learn, imbalanced-learn, DeepChem, Chemprop, RDKit,
transformers, Ray Tune, SHAP, UMAP. Trained on Imperial College London's PBS cluster on
RTX6000 GPUs, with the ensembles as array jobs.
